@.str.30 = private unnamed_addr constant [6813 x i8] c"\0A      Fetch a path from git. *args* can be a URL, in which case the HEAD\0A      of the repo at that URL is fetched. Otherwise, it can be an\0A      attribute with the following attributes (all except `url` optional):\0A\0A      - `url`\0A\0A        The URL of the repo.\0A\0A      - `name` (default: `source`)\0A\0A        The name of the directory the repo should be exported to in the store.\0A\0A      - `rev` (default: *the tip of `ref`*)\0A\0A        The [Git revision] to fetch.\0A        This is typically a commit hash.\0A\0A        [Git revision]: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-rev-parse#_specifying_revisions\0A\0A      - `ref` (default: `HEAD`)\0A\0A        The [Git reference] under which to look for the requested revision.\0A        This is often a branch or tag name.\0A\0A        [Git reference]: https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Git-Internals-Git-References\0A\0A        By default, the `ref` value is prefixed with `refs/heads/`.\0A        As of 2.3.0, Nix will not prefix `refs/heads/` if `ref` starts with `refs/`.\0A\0A      - `submodules` (default: `false`)\0A\0A        A Boolean parameter that specifies whether submodules should be checked out.\0A\0A      - `exportIgnore` (default: `true`)\0A\0A        A Boolean parameter that specifies whether `export-ignore` from `.gitattributes` should be applied.\0A        This approximates part of the `git archive` behavior.\0A\0A        Enabling this option is not recommended because it is unknown whether the Git developers commit to the reproducibility of `export-ignore` in newer Git versions.\0A\0A      - `shallow` (default: `false`)\0A\0A        Make a shallow clone when fetching the Git tree.\0A\0A      - `allRefs`\0A\0A        Whether to fetch all references of the repository.\0A        With this argument being true, it's possible to load a `rev` from *any* `ref`\0A        (by default only `rev`s from the specified `ref` are supported).\0A\0A      - `verifyCommit` (default: `true` if `publicKey` or `publicKeys` are provided, otherwise `false`)\0A\0A        Whether to check `rev` for a signature matching `publicKey` or `publicKeys`.\0A        If `verifyCommit` is enabled, then `fetchGit` cannot use a local repository with uncommitted changes.\0A        Requires the [`verified-fetches` experimental feature](@docroot@/contributing/experimental-features.md#xp-feature-verified-fetches).\0A\0A      - `publicKey`\0A\0A        The public key against which `rev` is verified if `verifyCommit` is enabled.\0A        Requires the [`verified-fetches` experimental feature](@docroot@/contributing/experimental-features.md#xp-feature-verified-fetches).\0A\0A      - `keytype` (default: `\22ssh-ed25519\22`)\0A\0A        The key type of `publicKey`.\0A        Possible values:\0A        - `\22ssh-dsa\22`\0A        - `\22ssh-ecdsa\22`\0A        - `\22ssh-ecdsa-sk\22`\0A        - `\22ssh-ed25519\22`\0A        - `\22ssh-ed25519-sk\22`\0A        - `\22ssh-rsa\22`\0A        Requires the [`verified-fetches` experimental feature](@docroot@/contributing/experimental-features.md#xp-feature-verified-fetches).\0A\0A      - `publicKeys`\0A\0A        The public keys against which `rev` is verified if `verifyCommit` is enabled.\0A        Must be given as a list of attribute sets with the following form:\0A        ```nix\0A        {\0A          key = \22<public key>\22;\0A          type = \22<key type>\22; # optional, default: \22ssh-ed25519\22\0A        }\0A        ```\0A        Requires the [`verified-fetches` experimental feature](@docroot@/contributing/experimental-features.md#xp-feature-verified-fetches).\0A\0A\0A      Here are some examples of how to use `fetchGit`.\0A\0A        - To fetch a private repository over SSH:\0A\0A          ```nix\0A          builtins.fetchGit {\0A            url = \22git@github.com:my-secret/repository.git\22;\0A            ref = \22master\22;\0A            rev = \22adab8b916a45068c044658c4158d81878f9ed1c3\22;\0A          }\0A          ```\0A\0A        - To fetch an arbitrary reference:\0A\0A          ```nix\0A          builtins.fetchGit {\0A            url = \22https://github.com/NixOS/nix.git\22;\0A            ref = \22refs/heads/0.5-release\22;\0A          }\0A          ```\0A\0A        - If the revision you're looking for is in the default branch of\0A          the git repository you don't strictly need to specify the branch\0A          name in the `ref` attribute.\0A\0A          However, if the revision you're looking for is in a future\0A          branch for the non-default branch you will need to specify the\0A          the `ref` attribute as well.\0A\0A          ```nix\0A          builtins.fetchGit {\0A            url = \22https://github.com/nixos/nix.git\22;\0A            rev = \22841fcbd04755c7a2865c51c1e2d3b045976b7452\22;\0A            ref = \221.11-maintenance\22;\0A          }\0A          ```\0A\0A          > **Note**\0A          >\0A          > It is nice to always specify the branch which a revision\0A          > belongs to. Without the branch being specified, the fetcher\0A          > might fail if the default branch changes. Additionally, it can\0A          > be confusing to try a commit from a non-default branch and see\0A          > the fetch fail. If the branch is specified the fault is much\0A          > more obvious.\0A\0A        - If the revision you're looking for is in the default branch of\0A          the git repository you may omit the `ref` attribute.\0A\0A          ```nix\0A          builtins.fetchGit {\0A            url = \22https://github.com/nixos/nix.git\22;\0A            rev = \22841fcbd04755c7a2865c51c1e2d3b045976b7452\22;\0A          }\0A          ```\0A\0A        - To fetch a specific tag:\0A\0A          ```nix\0A          builtins.fetchGit {\0A            url = \22https://github.com/nixos/nix.git\22;\0A            ref = \22refs/tags/1.9\22;\0A          }\0A          ```\0A\0A        - To fetch the latest version of a remote branch:\0A\0A          ```nix\0A          builtins.fetchGit {\0A            url = \22ssh://git@github.com/nixos/nix.git\22;\0A            ref = \22master\22;\0A          }\0A          ```\0A\0A        - To verify the commit signature:\0A\0A          ```nix\0A          builtins.fetchGit {\0A            url = \22ssh://git@github.com/nixos/nix.git\22;\0A            verifyCommit = true;\0A            publicKeys = [\0A                {\0A                  type = \22ssh-ed25519\22;\0A                  key = \22AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIArPKULJOid8eS6XETwUjO48/HKBWl7FTCK0Z//fplDi\22;\0A                }\0A            ];\0A          }\0A          ```\0A\0A          Nix will refetch the branch according to the [`tarball-ttl`](@docroot@/command-ref/conf-file.md#conf-tarball-ttl) setting.\0A\0A          This behavior is disabled in [pure evaluation mode](@docroot@/command-ref/conf-file.md#conf-pure-eval).\0A\0A        - To fetch the content of a checked-out work directory:\0A\0A          ```nix\0A          builtins.fetchGit ./work-dir\0A          ```\0A\0A      If the URL points to a local directory, and no `ref` or `rev` is\0A      given, `fetchGit` will use the current content of the checked-out\0A      files, even if they are not committed or added to Git's index. It will\0A      only consider files added to the Git repository, as listed by `git ls-files`.\0A    \00", align 1
